1
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:16,500
The future begins.

2
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:27,000
Star Trek is a science fiction, action, adventure film directed by J.J. Abrams.

3
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:37,500
The fate of the galaxy rests in the hands of bitter rivals.

4
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:48,000
One, James Kirk, is a delinquent, thrill-seeking Iowa farm boy.

5
00:15:52,500 --> 00:15:58,500
The other, Spock, a Vulcan, was raised in a
logic-based society that rejects all emotion.

6
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:09,000
As fiery instinct clashes with calm reason, their unlikely but powerful partnership is the only thing capable
of leading their crew through unimaginable danger, boldly going where no one has gone before.

7
00:22:13,500 --> 00:22:19,500
The human adventure has begun again.

8
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:30,000
Cadet James T. Kirk, played by Chris Pine.

9
00:28:34,500 --> 00:28:40,500
Commander Spock, played by Zachary Quinto.

10
00:31:45,000 --> 00:31:51,000
Ambassador Spock, played by Leonard Nimoy.

11
00:34:55,500 --> 00:35:01,500
Captain Nero, played by Eric Bana.

12
00:38:06,000 --> 00:38:12,000
Captain Christopher Pike, played by Bruce Greenwood.

13
00:41:16,500 --> 00:41:22,500
Dr. Leonard 'Bones' McCoy, played by Karl Urban.

14
00:44:27,000 --> 00:44:33,000
Star Trek is a 2009 American science fiction action film directed by J.

15
00:47:37,500 --> 00:47:43,500
Abrams, and written by Roberto Orci and Alex Kurtzman.

16
00:50:48,000 --> 00:50:54,000
It is the 11th film in the Star Trek franchise, and is also a reboot that features the main characters of the
original Star Trek television series portrayed by a new cast, as the first in the rebooted film series.

17
00:53:58,500 --> 00:54:04,500
The film follows James T.

18
00:57:09,000 --> 00:57:15,000
Kirk (Chris Pine) and Spock (Zachary Quinto) aboard the USS Enterprise as they combat Nero (Eric
Bana), a Romulan from their future who threatens the United Federation of Planets.

19
01:00:19,500 --> 01:00:25,500
The story takes place in an alternate reality that features
both an alternate birth location for James T.

20
01:03:30,000 --> 01:03:36,000
Kirk and further alterations in history stemming from the time travel of
both Nero and the original series Spock (Leonard Nimoy).

21
01:06:40,500 --> 01:06:46,500
The alternate reality was created in an attempt to free the film and the franchise from
established continuity constraints while simultaneously preserving original story elements.

22
01:09:51,000 --> 01:09:57,000
The idea for a prequel film which would follow the Star Trek characters during their time in
Starfleet Academy was first discussed by series creator Gene Roddenberry in 1968.

23
01:13:01,500 --> 01:13:07,500
The concept resurfaced in the late 1980s, when it was postulated by Harve Bennett as a possible plotline for what would
become Star Trek VI: The Undiscovered Country, but it was rejected in favor of other projects by Roddenberry.

24
01:16:12,000 --> 01:16:18,000
Following the critical and commercial failure of Star Trek: Nemesis and the cancellation of Star Trek: Enterprise, the franchise's executive producer Rick
Berman and screenwriter Erik Jendresen wrote an unproduced film titled Star Trek: The Beginning, which would take place after Enterprise.

25
01:19:22,500 --> 01:19:28,500
After the separation of Viacom and CBS Corporation in 2005, former Paramount Pictures president
Gail Berman convinced CBS to allow Paramount to produce a new film in the franchise.

26
01:22:33,000 --> 01:22:39,000
Orci and Kurtzman were soon approached to write the film,
and Abrams was approached to direct it.

27
01:25:43,500 --> 01:25:49,500
Kurtzman and Orci used inspiration from novels and graduate
school dissertations, as well as the series itself.

28
01:28:54,000 --> 01:29:00,000
Principal photography occurred between November 2007 and March
2008, in locations around California and Utah.

29
01:32:04,500 --> 01:32:10,500
Abrams wanted to avoid using greenscreen, and
preferred sets and locations instead.

30
01:35:15,000 --> 01:35:21,000
Heavy secrecy surrounded the film's production and was under
the fake working title Corporate Headquarters.

31
01:38:25,500 --> 01:38:31,500
Industrial Light & Magic used digital ships for the film, as opposed to
miniatures used in most of the previous films in the franchise.

32
01:41:36,000 --> 01:41:42,000
Production for the film concluded by the end of 2008.

33
01:44:46,500 --> 01:44:52,500
Star Trek was heavily promoted in the months preceding its release; pre-release screenings for the
film premiered in select cities around the world, including Austin, Sydney, and Calgary.

34
01:47:57,000 --> 01:48:03,000
It was released in the United States on May 8, 2009, to critical acclaim.

35
01:51:07,500 --> 01:51:13,500
The film was a financial success, grossing over $385.7 million
worldwide against its $150 million production budget.

36
01:54:18,000 --> 01:54:24,000
It was nominated for several awards, including four at the 82nd Academy Awards,
winning Best Makeup—the only Academy Award a Star Trek film has won.

37
01:57:28,500 --> 01:57:34,500
It was followed by the sequels Star Trek Into Darkness
(2013) and Star Trek Beyond (2016).

38
02:00:39,000 --> 02:00:45,000
The film explores themes of spacecraft, teleportation, san
francisco, california, time travel, space mission.

39
02:03:49,500 --> 02:03:55,500
The tone is thought_provoking.
